#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""./configure: write the deployment's .env, or check it.

    ./configure                         ask a few questions, write .env
    ./configure --non-interactive --origin https://chat.example.org \\
                --admin-email ops@example.org [--preset team] [--tls acme] [--idp authelia]
    ./configure --check                 what is wrong with this install, all at once
    ./configure --set KEY=VALUE ...     change values in .env (no questions)
    ./configure --unset KEY ...         remove values from .env

It asks only what it cannot decide: the public origin, the administrator's
email, a preset, the TLS mode and the identity provider. It mints every
secret it can, writes .env at mode 0600 in the layout of .env.example, and
starts nothing.

Re-running it edits an existing .env. It never overwrites one: secrets are
kept, your own edits are kept unless the answer they depend on changed,
keys it does not know are carried over, and the previous file is saved as
.env.bak-<time>.

Python 3.9+, standard library only, one file: read it before running it.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

